umath/string_buffer.h: undo Haiku's <ctype.h> function-like macros

Haiku's <ctype.h> defines isalnum(), isalpha(), isdigit(), islower(), isspace() and isupper() as
function-like macros in C++ too (glibc defines them for C only). string_buffer.h declares Buffer
member functions of those names (`bool isalpha()`), which the preprocessor then expands:
"error: expected primary-expression before ')' token" at string_buffer.h:518. Undefining the
macros is enough: <ctype.h> still declares the functions, so any call of them still compiles.

The same change as HaikuPorts' numpy-2.5.3.patchset (Oscar Lesta, 2024-12-24); still needed for
2.5.4 (checked by compiling string_ufuncs.cpp without it). The proper fix is in Haiku: no
function-like ctype macros under __cplusplus. Applied by scripts/build-numpy.sh.

--- a/numpy/_core/src/umath/string_buffer.h
+++ b/numpy/_core/src/umath/string_buffer.h
@@ -14,6 +14,15 @@
 #include "string_fastsearch.h"
 #include "gil_utils.h"
 
+#ifdef __HAIKU__
+	#undef isalnum
+	#undef isalpha
+	#undef isdigit
+	#undef islower
+	#undef isspace
+	#undef isupper
+#endif
+
 #ifdef _MSC_VER
 // MSVC sometimes complains (C4715: "not all control paths return a value")
 // on switch statements over enum classes, even though all enum values are covered.
